    <statement><p>For the integral
<me>\int_0^{2} \int_0^{x} e^{x^2}\,dy\,dx,</me>
sketch the region of integration and evaluate the integral.</p>
<p>Your sketch should be approximately the same as one of the
graphs shown below; which is the correct region?
Graph <ul form="popup" name="AnSwEr0001"><li>
<p>?</p>
</li><li>
<p>1</p>
</li><li>
<p>2</p>
</li><li>
<p>3</p>
</li><li>
<p>4</p>
</li><li>
<p>5</p>
</li><li>
<p>6</p>
</li></ul></p>
<p>Then
<m>\int_0^{2} \int_0^{x} e^{x^2}\,dy\,dx =</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p><alert> Note:</alert> the value of the integral needs to be correct to
two decimal places.</p>
<p>Graphs:</p>
<tabular xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" bottom="medium" left="medium" right="medium" top="medium">
<row>
<cell>1.
<image pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Michigan__Chap16Sec2__Q19__pg-image-1.png" width="33%"/>
</cell>
<cell>2.
<image pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Michigan__Chap16Sec2__Q19__pg-image-2.png" width="33%"/>
</cell>
<cell>3.
<image pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Michigan__Chap16Sec2__Q19__pg-image-3.png" width="33%"/>
</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>4.
<image pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Michigan__Chap16Sec2__Q19__pg-image-4.png" width="33%"/>
</cell>
<cell>5.
<image pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Michigan__Chap16Sec2__Q19__pg-image-5.png" width="33%"/>
</cell>
<cell>6.
<image pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Michigan__Chap16Sec2__Q19__pg-image-6.png" width="33%"/>
<p/></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{4}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\frac{1}{2}\mathopen{}\left(e^{4}-1\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>x=0</m> and <m>x=2</m>,
below by <m>y=0</m> and above by <m>y=x</m>.  Thus the correct region
of integration is graph 4.  Evaluating
the integral, we have
<me>\int_{0}^{2}\int_{0}^{x} e^{x^{2}}\, dy\, dx =
    \int_{0}^{2} e^{x^{2}} y\bigg|_{0}^{x}\, dx =
    \int_{0}^{2} x e^{x^{2}} \, dx =
    \frac{1}{2} e^{x^{2}}\bigg|_{0}^{2} =
    \frac{1}{2} (e^{4}-1).</me>




</solution>
